chapter "AMP"

(*
 * Sessions for the verified asymmetric-multiprocessing (AMP) work.
 * See ../../multicore-amp-plan.md for the phase plan; each phase adds one or a
 * few sessions here, each buildable green (no sorry) on its own.
 *
 * Fast iteration (see amp/README.md): every AMP session sits on top of an
 * already-built prerequisite whose heap is cached, so editing a phase's theory
 * rebuilds only that theory, not its dependencies.
 *)

(* Phase 1 -- AMP system model (spec only): the multicore composition of
   per-core abstract states.

   Parented on Access rather than on ASpec. That is not because Phase 1 needs
   l4v's access-control development -- it does not -- but because the ledger
   layers below (AMP_Integrity onwards) do, and Isabelle gives a session exactly
   ONE parent heap. With the AMP sessions on ASpec and the ledger sessions on
   Access, no session could have both, and any session needing both would have
   had to re-check one whole branch from source on every build: measured, that
   meant re-checking CParser and AutoCorres inside AMP_Overview every time it
   was edited. Putting Access at the root instead makes the whole tree one
   linear spine in which every session keeps its own cached heap. *)
session AMP_Model in "model" = Access +
  description \<open>Phase 1: AMP system model -- N single-core abstract states plus a static boot partition.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Model"

(* Phase 2 -- spatial partition invariant (B1-B3): each per-core kernel step
   preserves the partition. Builds on the cached AMP_Model heap. *)
session AMP_Spatial in "spatial" = AMP_Model +
  description \<open>Phase 2: spatial isolation -- a core's step touches only its owned frames; the writer/reader buffer mapping is asymmetric.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Spatial"

(* Phase 3 -- channel object + abstract operations (C1-C3): xchan_send and
   xchan_recv_ack, specified as atomic abstract steps mirroring IPC endpoints,
   shown to preserve the Phase 2 spatial partition invariant. Builds on the
   cached AMP_Spatial heap. *)
session AMP_Channel_A in "channel" = AMP_Spatial +
  description \<open>Phase 3: cross-core channel object -- xchan_send/xchan_recv_ack preserve the spatial partition invariant.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Channel_A"

(* Phase 4 (R half) -- channel refinement, design level: xchan_send_R and
   xchan_recv_ack_R are total deterministic functions over a machine-word
   status, shown via xchan_map_abs to refine (corres) Phase 3's abstract
   xchan_send/xchan_recv_ack -- mirrors Ipc_R, scoped down to a vertical
   slice since the channel needs none of Ipc_R's capability-transfer
   machinery. C-level (mirrors Ipc_C) is a separate later session. Builds on
   the cached AMP_Channel_A heap. *)
session AMP_Channel_R in "channel_r" = AMP_Concurrency +
  description \<open>Phase 4 (R half): design-level channel ops refine the Phase 3 abstract ops (corres).\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Channel_R"

(* Phase 4 (C half) -- channel refinement, C level: xchan_send_c and
   xchan_recv_ack_c, two minimal hand-written C functions (amp/channel_c/
   xchan.c) parsed and lifted via AutoCorres -- not full CRefine/ccorres,
   since this C exists nowhere in kernel_all.c yet (see the theory header
   for why) -- shown to correctly implement Phase 4 R half's xchan_send_R /
   xchan_recv_ack_R. Builds on the cached AMP_Channel_R heap plus the
   AutoCorres session. *)
session AMP_Channel_C in "channel_c" = AMP_Channel_R +
  sessions
    AutoCorres
  theories
    "AMP_Channel_C"

(* Phase 5 -- channel access control (D-spatial half of security): an
   explicit authority-graph vocabulary (mirrors Access.thy's auth_graph/
   pas_refined) derived from ap_channels, shown to match exactly what Phase
   2's MMU permission map (frame_perm) already enforces, and used to state
   send/recv authority-confinement theorems. Builds on the cached
   AMP_Channel_A heap -- this is the abstract-op level (mirrors Ipc_AC),
   independent of the R/C refinement chain, per the plan's "Reuses: Phase 2
   partition, Phase 3 abstract ops". *)
session AMP_Channel_AC in "channel_ac" = AMP_Channel_A +
  description \<open>Phase 5: channel access control -- the declared authority graph matches the enforced MMU permission map; send/recv are authority-confined.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Channel_AC"

(* Phase 5.5 -- message content, integrity & confidentiality (M1-M4): gives
   the channel protocol a message operand (xchan_send_msg/xchan_recv), then
   proves the receiver reads exactly what the sender sent (M2, under the
   named sender_quiescent locale -- Phase 6's D2 discharges it), that a send
   is invisible to any core outside the channel for any message value (M3,
   unconditional), and that the acknowledgement's real backward flow is
   bounded to one channel-status bit with zero content (M4). Runs in
   parallel with Phase 6; needs nothing from it. Builds on the cached
   AMP_Channel_AC heap (Phase 5), per the plan's "Reuses: Phases 1, 2, 3, 5". *)
session AMP_Message in "message" = AMP_Channel_AC +
  description \<open>Phase 5.5: message content -- the receiver reads what the sender sent (under sender_quiescent); a send is invisible outside its declared endpoints; the ack's reverse flow is bounded to one status bit.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Message"

(* Phase 5.75 -- channel thread ownership (T1-T4): narrows write authority on
   a channel buffer from the CORE (Phase 2's frame_perm) to one designated
   owning THREAD per endpoint, and thereby discharges Phase 5.5's
   sender_quiescent locale outright (via a sublocale relationship) -- trading
   it in the TCB for a single new attribution assumption, A-THR, packaged as
   the thread_ownership locale. Builds on the cached AMP_Message heap, per the
   plan's "Reuses: Phases 1, 2, 3, 5, 5.5". *)
session AMP_Thread in "thread" = AMP_Message +
  description \<open>Phase 5.75: channel thread ownership -- narrows write authority from core to owning thread, discharging sender_quiescent (traded for A-THR).\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Thread"

(* Phase 6 -- concurrency core (D1-D3): an explicit-state interleaving
   model (cstep/csteps) over a small control skeleton (the status word, the
   sender's outstanding-write flag, the receiver's progress), showing by
   forward simulation that a genuinely interleaved trace -- with an
   arbitrary amount of other-thread activity interposed anywhere -- still
   yields a real xchan_send_msg, a real xchan_recv_ack, and message
   integrity (D1), citing Phase 5.75's ownership result rather than
   reproving it (D2). Builds on the cached AMP_Thread heap. *)
session AMP_Concurrency in "concurrency" = AMP_Thread +
  description \<open>Phase 6: concurrency core -- an interleaved trace with arbitrary other-thread activity refines the atomic send/recv-ack protocol.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Concurrency"

(* Integrity connection layer -- the first payment on the plan's assumption
   ledger: `amp_step`, which every phase from 2 onward carries as a hypothesis,
   is shown to BE seL4's own `integrity` (Access.thy) projected onto frames.
   This is the first session that needs l4v's access-control development, which
   is why Access sits at the root of the whole AMP spine (see AMP_Model above).
   Scope is deliberately the bridging lemma only; the per-core invariant that
   discharges call_kernel_integrity's preconditions is AMP_Invariant, and the
   lift to whole ADT_A steps is AMP_ADT. *)
session AMP_Integrity in "integrity" = AMP_Channel_C +
  description \<open>Integrity connection: amp_step is seL4's integrity_mem projected onto frames, under a one-label-per-core PAS.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Integrity"

(* Per-core invariant layer -- the second payment on the ledger. States, in the
   boot partition's own vocabulary, what it means for a kernel state to realise
   the declared partition (`amp_config`), and proves that any such state
   satisfies seL4's `pas_refined` for that core's PAS. This is the alternative
   to l4v's per-configuration route (ExampleSystem.thy, 1148 lines for a
   two-thread system), and it is available only because the PAS is
   core-granular. Builds on the cached AMP_Integrity heap. *)
session AMP_Invariant in "invariant" = AMP_Integrity +
  description \<open>Per-core invariant: a state realising the declared partition satisfies pas_refined.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Invariant"

(* ADT step layer -- the third and last payment on the ledger for `amp_step`.
   Lifts the integrity connection from `call_kernel` and `do_user_op` to whole
   steps of seL4's abstract system `ADT_A`, whose six-clause automaton also
   covers interrupt polling and three mode changes. `amp_step_of_ADT_A` is what
   turns `amp_step` from a hypothesis of Phases 2-6 into a conclusion. Kept
   separate from AMP_Invariant so that editing the ADT lift does not rebuild the
   expensive `pas_refined` reduction. Builds on the cached AMP_Invariant heap. *)
session AMP_ADT in "adt" = AMP_Invariant +
  description \<open>ADT step layer: every ADT_A step of a core is an amp_step of that core.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_ADT"

(* Overview -- cumulative, machine-checked index of every phase's headline
   result: a plain-English paragraph directly beside the exact formal
   statement, re-derived from the real theorem so drift breaks the build. Read
   this file, not any phase's own theory, if you only want to know WHAT has
   been proved. Updated once per phase (plan section 2.5). Builds on the cached
   AMP_ADT heap, which is the end of the spine and so carries every earlier
   session transitively -- including, since the restructuring above, both the
   C-refinement branch and the l4v access-control branch. *)
session AMP_Overview in "overview" = AMP_ADT +
  description \<open>Cumulative specification index: every phase's headline theorem, restated next to its plain-English meaning and re-derived from the real proof.\<close>
  theories
    "AMP_Overview"
